Z||000006||Carrolles||05-17-2001||08:18 AM||||With the engine off, being able to turn the compressor pulley does not tell you whether the compressor is locked up or not because the clutch is not engaged.  You are simply spinning the drive end of the clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000007||jaguiler||05-17-2001||09:26 AM||||I put enough gas in to bring the low side up to about 30 - 40 psi..then took some out to leave it at 25 psi.<BR>I was able to turn the compressor by hand. Even if that is not an indication that it works.  I figured that fact that it spins when I short the switch on the front mean that it works.  Right?<BR><P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000008||Indiana_resto_guy||05-17-2001||10:00 AM||||Well, when you turn the outside of the compressor pulley it is not an indication that it will work. All that does is tell you that the clutch isn't frozen.<BR>I'm guessing that you have put an oil charge(at least 6 oz.'s) and an 'O' ring conditioner into the system already. Next you need to start the car and dump (upside down) a couple of 12oz. cans of 134A into the system. This should be enough to energize the system to start the compressor cycling. After that take another 12oz. can and add gas only (right side up) to proper operating levels on the low pressure (suction) side.<BR>If after the oil charge and conditioner with the first 2 cans the compressor will not operate, you have problems with the low pressure switches.<BR>Bring your system up to the first step, since I don't know what it took to get where you are now, and see if your compressor will energize.<BR>You may also want to install a 4 oz. can of leak detector/sealer in as well if your system was bleed of due to natural causes.||||reg||1
Z||000009||jaguiler||05-17-2001||10:05 AM||||Great info! - now what are the low pressure switches?  Where can I find them?  Hanyes does not tell me too much about it......<BR>Does anyone know of a troubleshooting page for the air conditioning?<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||05-17-2001||12:18 PM||||Compressor low pressure switch is located on the back of the A/C compressor. It's the White on one a single wire connector. Red one is high pressure.||||reg||1
Z||000011||jaguiler||05-17-2001||12:23 PM||||is there a way to test or troubleshoot the system to narrow down my options?  Is the switch readily available? Easy to change or do I have to discharge everything?<P>------------------<BR>______<BR>__|aguiler||65.8.46.102||reg||1
Z||000012||jaguiler||05-18-2001||12:27 AM||||"I'm guessing that you have put an oil charge(at least 6 oz.'s) and an 'O' ring conditioner into the system already. Next you need to start the car and dump (upside down) a couple of 12oz. cans of 134A into the system. This should be enough to energize the system to start the compressor cycling. After that take another 12oz. can and add gas only (right side up) to proper operating levels on the low pressure (suction) side."<P><BR>-  if I do this won't I go over the rec. PSI? or is it that loading two cans right side up will not bring up pressure.... but putting one in upide down will push up the pressure?  I'm confused.. I would hate to overcharge the system...||||reg||1
